Assuming the XML data is in a file named "currency.xml", here's some Java code that uses the DOM parser to retrieve the "Full Name" value for the currency used in Europe:
```java
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory;
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilder;
import org.w3c.dom.Document;
import org.w3c.dom.NodeList;
import org.w3c.dom.Element;
try {
    // create a document builder factory
    DocumentBuilderFactory factory = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
    // create a document builder
    DocumentBuilder builder = factory.newDocumentBuilder();
    // parse the XML file
    Document doc = builder.parse("currency.xml");
    // get the list of currency elements
    NodeList currencyList = doc.getElementsByTagName("currency");
    // loop through the currency elements
    for (int i = 0; i < currencyList.getLength(); i++) {
        Element currency = (Element) currencyList.item(i);
        // check if this currency is used in Europe
        if (currency.getAttribute("zone").equals("Europe")) {
            // get the full name element
            Element fullName = (Element) currency.getElementsByTagName("FullName").item(0);
            // print the full name value
            System.out.println("Full Name: " + fullName.getTextContent());
            // we found the currency we were looking for, so break out of the loop
            break;
        }
    }
} catch (Exception e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
}
```
Note that this code assumes that there is only one currency used in Europe in the XML file. If there are multiple currencies used in Europe, you may need to modify the code to handle that case.

