In Java, we can calculate the motion of an object using the laws of motion, specifically Newton's second law of motion. This law states that the force acting on an object is equal to its mass times its acceleration. By knowing the initial position, velocity, and force acting upon the object, we can use this law to calculate its motion.
Here is an example code snippet that demonstrates this law:
```
// Define the initial position, velocity, and force
double initialPosition = 0.0;
double initialVelocity = 10.0;
double force = 100.0;
// Define the mass of the object
double mass = 5.0;
// Define the time interval
double timeInterval = 0.1;
// Calculate the acceleration using Newton's second law
double acceleration = force / mass;
// Calculate the new position and velocity using the kinematic equations
double newPosition = initialPosition + initialVelocity * timeInterval + 0.5 * acceleration * timeInterval * timeInterval;
double newVelocity = initialVelocity + acceleration * timeInterval;
// Print the results
System.out.println("New position: " + newPosition);
System.out.println("New velocity: " + newVelocity);
```
In this example, we define the initial position, velocity, and force acting upon the object. We also define the mass of the object and the time interval. We then use Newton's second law to calculate the acceleration, and use the kinematic equations to calculate the new position and velocity. Finally, we print out the results.

