To classify a given text as either "Business" or "Politics", you can use Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques to analyze the text and extract relevant features. Here is a sample Java code that uses Stanford CoreNLP library to classify the text:
```java
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.CoreAnnotations;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.CoreLabel;
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.Annotation;
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.StanfordCoreNLP;
import edu.stanford.nlp.util.CoreMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Properties;
public class TextClassifier {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String text = "The latest policy signed by the president aims to combat climate change by implementing more specific regulations and incentives to increase the adoption of renewable energy sources and decrease greenhouse gas emissions from industrial sources.";
        // Set up Stanford CoreNLP pipeline
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.setProperty("annotators", "tokenize, ssplit, pos");
        StanfordCoreNLP pipeline = new StanfordCoreNLP(props);
        // Create an empty Annotation just with the given text
        Annotation document = new Annotation(text);
        // Run all Annotators on this text
        pipeline.annotate(document);
        // Extract POS tags from the text
        List<CoreMap> sentences = document.get(CoreAnnotations.SentencesAnnotation.class);
        for (CoreMap sentence : sentences) {
            for (CoreLabel token : sentence.get(CoreAnnotations.TokensAnnotation.class)) {
                String pos = token.get(CoreAnnotations.PartOfSpeechAnnotation.class);
                if (pos.startsWith("NN")) { // If the token is a noun
                    if (pos.startsWith("NNP")) { // If the noun is a proper noun
                        // Check if the noun is related to politics
                        if (isRelatedToPolitics(token.originalText())) {
                            System.out.println("The text is related to Politics");
                            return;
                        }
                    } else { // If the noun is a common noun
                        // Check if the noun is related to business
                        if (isRelatedToBusiness(token.originalText())) {
                            System.out.println("The text is related to Business");
                            return;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        System.out.println("The text is not related to Business or Politics");
    }
    private static boolean isRelatedToPolitics(String text) {
        // Add your own logic to determine if the text is related to politics
        // For example, you can use a list of political keywords or a machine learning model
        return text.contains("president") || text.contains("policy") || text.contains("climate change");
    }
    private static boolean isRelatedToBusiness(String text) {
        // Add your own logic to determine if the text is related to business
        // For example, you can use a list of business keywords or a machine learning model
        return text.contains("regulations") || text.contains("incentives") || text.contains("renewable energy sources");
    }
}
```
In this code, we first set up a Stanford CoreNLP pipeline to tokenize the text, split it into sentences, and tag each token with its Part-Of-Speech (POS) tag. Then, we iterate over each token in the text and check if it is a noun. If the noun is a proper noun, we check if it is related to politics. If the noun is a common noun, we check if it is related to business. Finally, we print the appropriate category based on the analysis. 
Note that the `isRelatedToPolitics` and `isRelatedToBusiness` methods are just placeholders and you need to implement your own logic to determine if a given text is related to politics or business. You can use a list of keywords or a machine learning model to achieve this task.

