To calculate the Levenshtein Distance between the two strings given in the XML format, you can first extract the strings from the XML and then pass them to the `levenshteinDistance` method. Here's an example Java code snippet that demonstrates this:
```java
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory;
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilder;
import org.w3c.dom.Document;
import org.w3c.dom.NodeList;
import org.w3c.dom.Node;
import org.w3c.dom.Element;
public class LevenshteinDistanceCalculator {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    try {
      // Parse the XML data
      DocumentBuilderFactory dbFactory = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
      DocumentBuilder dBuilder = dbFactory.newDocumentBuilder();
      Document doc = dBuilder.parse("data.xml");
      // Extract the strings from the XML
      NodeList string1List = doc.getElementsByTagName("string1");
      Element string1Element = (Element) string1List.item(0);
      String string1 = string1Element.getTextContent();
      NodeList string2List = doc.getElementsByTagName("string2");
      Element string2Element = (Element) string2List.item(0);
      String string2 = string2Element.getTextContent();
      // Compute the Levenshtein Distance
      int distance = levenshteinDistance(string1, string2);
      System.out.println("Levenshtein Distance: " + distance);
    } catch (Exception e) {
      e.printStackTrace();
    }
  }
  public static int levenshteinDistance(String s, String t) {
    int[][] distance = new int[s.length() + 1][t.length() + 1];
    for (int i = 0; i <= s.length(); i++) {
      distance[i][0] = i;
    }
    for (int j = 1; j <= t.length(); j++) {
      distance[0][j] = j;
    }
    for (int i = 1; i <= s.length(); i++) {
      for (int j = 1; j <= t.length(); j++) {
        int cost = (s.charAt(i - 1) == t.charAt(j - 1)) ? 0 : 1;
        distance[i][j] = Math.min(Math.min(distance[i - 1][j] + 1, distance[i][j - 1] + 1), distance[i - 1][j - 1] + cost);
      }
    }
    return distance[s.length()][t.length()];
  }
}
```
Assuming the XML data is stored in a file named `data.xml`, this code extracts the two strings, passes them to the `levenshteinDistance` method, and prints the resulting Levenshtein Distance to the console.
The `levenshteinDistance` method itself is the same as the Java code you provided, and it computes the Levenshtein Distance between two input strings using dynamic programming.

