How can I write a Java program that summarizes a given poem in two sentences?
Here's an example code that uses the Stanford CoreNLP library to summarize a poem:
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Properties;
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.Annotation;
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.StanfordCoreNLP;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.CoreAnnotations.SentencesAnnotation;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.CoreAnnotations.TextAnnotation;
import edu.stanford.nlp.neural.rnn.RNNCoreAnnotations;
import edu.stanford.nlp.sentiment.SentimentCoreAnnotations;
import edu.stanford.nlp.util.CoreMap;
public class PoemSummarizer {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    String poem = "Two roads diverged in a wood, and I—\nI took the one less traveled by,\nAnd that has made all the difference.";
    Properties props = new Properties();
    props.setProperty("annotators", "tokenize,ssplit,pos,lemma,ner,parse,coref,sentiment");
    StanfordCoreNLP pipeline = new StanfordCoreNLP(props);
    Annotation document = new Annotation(poem);
    pipeline.annotate(document);
    List<CoreMap> sentences = document.get(SentencesAnnotation.class);
    int[] sentimentScores = new int[sentences.size()];
    for (int i = 0; i < sentences.size(); i++) {
      CoreMap sentence = sentences.get(i);
      sentimentScores[i] = RNNCoreAnnotations.getPredictedClass(sentence.get(SentimentCoreAnnotations.SentimentAnnotatedTree.class));
    }
    int totalSentimentScore = 0;
    for (int score : sentimentScores) {
      totalSentimentScore += score;
    }
    double averageSentimentScore = (double) totalSentimentScore / sentimentScores.length;
    String summary = "";
    for (CoreMap sentence : sentences) {
      double sentimentScore = (double) RNNCoreAnnotations.getPredictedClass(sentence.get(SentimentCoreAnnotations.SentimentAnnotatedTree.class));
      if (sentimentScore > averageSentimentScore) {
        summary += sentence.get(TextAnnotation.class) + " ";
      }
    }
    System.out.println("Summary: " + summary);
  }
}
This code uses sentiment analysis to select the two sentences with the highest sentiment score, which are assumed to be the most important sentences in the poem. Note that this is just one approach to summarizing a poem, and other methods may be more effective depending on the poem and the desired level of detail in the summary.

